    "evidence_text": "For this task the Agenda-Based User Simulator (ABUS) is often used.\n\nFurthermore, the trained policies are tested on real users.\n\nIntroduction\tSpoken Dialogue Systems (SDS) allow human-computer interaction using natural speech.\n\nTask-oriented dialogue systems, the\n\nfocus of this work, help users achieve goals such as finding restaurants or booking flights  .\n\nTeaching a system how to respond appropriately in a task-oriented setting is non-trivial.\n\nIn this framework, the system learns by a trial and error process governed by a reward function.\n\nIn this paper the Neural User Simulator (NUS) is introduced which outputs natural language and whose behaviour is learned from a corpus.\n\nThis allows the model to be deployed for training more sophisticated DM policies.\n\nTo achieve this, a method is proposed that transforms the\n\ngoal-labels of the used dataset (DSTC2) into labels whose behaviour can be replicated during deployment.\n\nThis means to train on one model and to test on the other.\n\nThe remainder of this paper is organised as follows.\n\nSection 3 describes the motivation for the NUS and discusses related work.\n\nSections 5 and 6 present the experimental setup\n\ninteractions, this is impractical.\n\nThis makes common practices such as hyper-parameter optimization prohibitively expensive.\n\nThus, it is natural to try to learn from a dataset which needs to be recorded only once, but can be used over and over again.\n\nHowever, even if the size of the corpus is large enough the optimal dialogue strategy is likely not to be contained within it.\n\nA solution is to transform the static corpus into a dynamic tool: a user simulator.\n\nThe US is trained using supervised learning since the aim is for it to learn typical user behaviour.\n\nFor the DM, however, we want optimal behaviour which is why supervised learning cannot be used.\n\nThe DM's policy is optimised using the feedback given by either the user simulator or a\n\nseparate evaluator.\n\nIt has the advantage of being purely probabilistic and domain-independent.\n\n,  attempted to overcome goal inconsistency by proposing a graph-based model.\n\nHowever, developing the graph structure requires extensive domain-specific knowledge.\n\nA Markov Model is also used by  .\n\nIt uses a large feature vector to describe the user's current state, which helps to compensate for the Markov assumption.\n\nHowever, the model is not conditioned on any goal.\n\nTherefore, it is not used\n\nto train a dialogue policy since it is impossible to determine whether the user goal was fulfilled.\n\ncast user simulation as an inverse reinforcement learning problem where the user is modelled as a decision-making agent.\n\nmodelled user simulation as a sequence-to-sequence task.\n\nThe model can keep track of the dialogue history and user behaviour is learned entirely from data.\n\nABUS was not.\n\nThe aforementioned work focuses on user simulation at the semantic level.\n\nMultiple issues arise from this approach.\n\nFirstly, annotating the user-response with the correct semantics is costly.\n\nMore data could be collected, if the US were to output natural language.\n\nWhen the error model matches the characteristics of the speech recogniser more accurately, the SDS performs better  .\n\nspoken words and not their semantics  .\n\nNeural User Simulator\tAn overview of the NUS is given in Figure FIGREF2 .\n\nAt the start of a dialogue a random goal INLINEFORM0 is generated by the Goal Generator.\n\nThe possibilities for INLINEFORM1 are defined by the ontology.\n\nThis vector is appended to the Feature History INLINEFORM7 .\n\nAs in Figure\n\nstrategy learned with a good user model still performs well when tested on poor user models.\n\nThis choice of policies is motivated\n\nIt thus needs less labelling of the training data than User Simulators that generate a response in semantic form.\n\nThe NUS excelled on both evaluation tasks.\n\nPawe\u0142 Budzianowski is supported by the EPSRC and Toshiba Research Europe Ltd.",
